# Target C: a macroscopic one-deficit cloud with diffuse strain and no vacancy

Date: 2026-10-07. Authority: audit.75.
Classification: **regression**, with a proved candidate exact competitor.
This family is explicitly nonglobal and is not a Level I disproof.

## 1. What fails and the surviving theorem

One area-unit of Oler deficit does not by itself create a discrete vacancy
or even a nonzero finite combinatorial defect. There is an unbounded
macroscopic convex-unit-wall family at exactly that deficit whose entire
Delaunay triangulation has valence-six interior vertices and no bad
triangle at the fixed thresholds of the preceding defect theorem.
Its excess is distributed metric strain. The strongest surviving
statement is the completion/defect-budget alternative **with its metric
budget retained**, not a claim that all missing capacity resides at the
exceptional vertices.

This regression does not contradict the specified six-direction curved
wall theorem. Its wall is a straight parallelogram, and its interior
points have actual contacts rather than being canonical inactive isolates.
It lacks global optimality. Those omitted hypotheses are explicit.

## 2. Exact unbounded family

For every integer M>=3 put

`h_M=(sqrt(3)/2)*(1+1/M^2)`,
`c_M=sqrt(M^4-6*M^2-3)/(2*M^2)>0`,
`u=(1,0)`, `v=(c_M,h_M)`.

One has c_M^2+h_M^2=1 and 0<c_M<1/2, so the angle between the
unit vectors is strictly between pi/3 and pi/2. Define

`A_M={i*u+j*v:0<=i,j<=M}`.

Every lattice difference has squared length
`i^2+j^2+2*c_M*i*j`. If i,j have the same sign it is at least
i^2+j^2. If both are nonzero with opposite signs, write their absolute
values as a,b; its squared length is
`(a-b)^2+2*(1-c_M)*a*b>1`.
Consequently the only unit contacts are the horizontal and vertical
nearest-neighbour edges. The contact graph is a square grid, hence
triangle-free, with 2*M*(M+1) contacts. There are (M+1)^2 retained labels.

The 4M boundary vertices form a convex unit wall. The interior population
is z=(M-1)^2. Its area-form full-wall Oler slack is

`area(F_M)-sqrt(3)/4*(2*z+4*M-2)`
`=M^2*h_M-(sqrt(3)/2)*M^2=sqrt(3)/2`.

Thus Oler permits exactly one point beyond the current count. Both area
and population are quadratic in M, and perimeter/diameter are linear;
this is not a thin or bounded regression.

## 3. Saturation and diffuse metric excess

The M^2 unit rhombi tile F_M. Each is covered by the open unit disks
about its four corners. One way to check this is to split it on the
short diagonal: both triangles are acute with circumradius squared
`1/[2*(1+c_M)]<1/2`. The maximum nearest-vertex distance within an
acute triangle is its circumradius (or a boundary half-edge length,
which is smaller here). Therefore no additional unit-separated point
can be inserted anywhere in the closed wall.

That short-diagonal triangulation is Delaunay. Across the diagonal the
two opposite angles sum to twice the rhombus angle, below pi; across a
horizontal or vertical internal edge the opposite-angle sum is pi minus
that angle, also below pi. All its 2M^2 triangles are acute, its interior
valences are six, and each triangle's exact area excess is

`sigma_T=sqrt(3)/(4*M^2)`.

For M>=7 every sigma_T<1/100. Hence the bounded-defect theorem's
exceptional set can be empty despite total excess sqrt(3)/2. The
equilateral development is the full M-by-M lattice parallelogram:
it has the same number of vertices, with no missing lattice site.
The strain has been spread over every triangle.

No impossibility of a different nonlocal rearrangement is asserted.
Saturation means no one-site insertion at these fixed old coordinates,
not maximum packet cardinality in the wall or global packing optimality.

## 4. Complete strict sub-diameter competitor

Replace every source label by the unit square grid
`B_M={(i,j):0<=i,j<=M}`. Its cardinality is exactly (M+1)^2, every
lower distance is at least one, and its contact graph is triangle-free.
Its diameter squared is 2M^2. The old diameter is attained on the
long corner diagonal and has square

`D_M^2=2*M^2*(1+c_M)=2*M^2+sqrt(M^4-6*M^2-3)>2*M^2`.

All old labels are replaced; there is no omitted retained-old constraint.
Every inserted-to-inserted lower pair is checked by its integer squared
distance, and every new upper pair is strictly below the actual old D_M.
No new pair can be an old actual diameter. Contacts and triangles are
counted exactly, and cardinality is unchanged. Thus the whole-source
competitor has strictly smaller diameter for every M>=3.

This proves the regression family is nonglobal even though it is
macroscopic, exactly one Oler point below the bound, saturated at its
old coordinates, and locally has the required near-equilateral metric
and valence pattern. Global optimality and the inactive-isolate premise
must provide additional information in any source-level application.

The exact verifier checks complete finite old/new pair ledgers at
M=3,4,7,10 using signs in Q(sqrt(M^4-6M^2-3)); the universal proof is
the algebra above. It does not certify global optimality, and does not
advertise this explicitly beaten family as a disproof of Level I.
